    bio: "Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz was born in Leipzig in 1646, the son of a professor of moral philosophy, and he seems to have inherited an almost limitless appetite for ideas. By the time he was twenty, he had mastered the philosophical canon, trained in law, and begun developing mathematical theories that would eventually place him among the founders of calculus — a distinction he would share, bitterly and controversially, with Isaac Newton. Unlike Newton, who worked in monastic isolation, Leibniz was a man of courts and letters: diplomat, librarian, historian, and one of the most prolific correspondents in European intellectual history.\n\nHis invention of calculus — developed independently of Newton in the 1670s — was only the most famous of his contributions to mathematics. He devised the binary number system that underlies all modern computing, improved the mechanical calculator, and developed a system of notation for calculus that proved so elegant and practical that it is still the notation used today, rather than Newton\'s own. He also dreamed of a universal logical language — a 'characteristica universalis' — that would reduce all human reasoning to calculation, a vision that would find its fulfilment, three centuries later, in the digital computer.\n\nAs a philosopher, Leibniz stands alongside Descartes and Spinoza as one of the great rationalists of the seventeenth century. His metaphysics centred on the theory of monads — irreducible units of reality, each a kind of self-contained universe — and his famous claim that we live in 'the best of all possible worlds,' a proposition that Voltaire would mercilessly satirise in Candide. His theology was earnest rather than cynical: he genuinely believed that God, being perfect, could only have created the best possible universe, and he spent much of his life trying to reconcile Protestant and Catholic Christianity in an era of bitter confessional division.\n\nLeibniz died in 1716, largely forgotten by the courts he had served and ignored at the time of his passing — his secretary was reportedly the only person to attend his funeral. The priority dispute with Newton had damaged his reputation in Britain, and he had never secured the permanent royal patronage he sought. But history has been generous. The machine he sketched, the notation he invented, and the logical dream he pursued have shaped the modern world more profoundly than almost any of his more celebrated contemporaries. He is, in many respects, the philosopher of the digital age — born three hundred years too soon."
